# Line endings are pinned in the repository, not left to each developer's
# `core.autocrlf`.
#
# Why this file exists rather than being left alone: `.prettierrc` sets
# `endOfLine: "lf"`, and on Windows the git default is `core.autocrlf = true`.
# With both in force and no `.gitattributes`, the working tree here is LF
# (Prettier wrote it), git stores LF, and **a fresh clone on the same machine
# checks out CRLF** - because autocrlf rewrites on checkout. Every file would
# then fail `prettier --check`, and `npm run check` would fail on a clean
# checkout while passing on the machine that produced it.
#
# That is the same shape as the five tooling bugs already found in this
# project: a gate that reports green locally and means nothing elsewhere. It is
# caught by a fresh clone, which is not something anyone does on a normal day.
# Worth fixing precisely because it is invisible until someone else clones.
#
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree on every platform, which is what the
# format gate requires. It does not affect the bytes stored in the repository,
# which git always normalises to LF for text files.

# Normalise text files to LF in the working tree, on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# These are not text. Without this, `text=auto` runs them through newline
# normalisation, and a PNG that happens to contain a CRLF-looking byte pair
# would be silently corrupted in the working tree.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp3 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
*.p8 binary
*.p12 binary

# Lockfiles are machine-generated and are not ours to reformat. They stay LF
# like everything else, but a merge conflict in one is resolved by regenerating
# it rather than by hand, and `.gitattributes` marks it as merge=ours so a
# casual conflict resolution cannot corrupt resolution data.
package-lock.json merge=ours

# `.expo/types/` is machine-written by Expo Router's type generation, is
# machine-ignored in `.prettierignore` and ESLint, and should never reach a
# merge. Ignoring it here as well means a machine that somehow lacks the
# `.gitignore` rule still cannot commit it.
.expo/ export-ignore
